  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "Sale & Purchase: The second-hand market was busy this week, with fifteen vessels reported sold or \ncommitted for further trading, nine dry and six wet. In the bulker sector, Greek buyers paid USD 49 mill for \nthe scrubber-fitted, 2017-built Capesize WILLIAM OLDENDORFF (179,120 dwt, Jinhai/China) basis forward \ndelivery in Q1 2027, setting a new benchmark. The 2011-built, scrubber-fitted Post-Panamax CARL \nOLDENDORFF (93,028 dwt, Taizhou CATIC/China) changed hands for USD 16.5 mill, more than USD 1.5 mill \nabove September's sale of the LC MILADY (93,758 dwt, built 2011 SWS/China) at high USD 14 mill. In the \nPanamax segment, Chinese buyers paid region high USD 13 mill for the Japanese-built AMOY SUNNY (76,598 \ndwt, 2006 Imabari) with her special survey recently passed, a firm price for her age, while the 2001 DSME-\nbuilt BAO NENG 16 (74,492 dwt) fetched a strong USD 9.294 mill at an online auction. In the geared sizes, \nSouth Korean buyers acquired the 2012 HMD-built, Tier II Supramax UNITY MELODY (55,705 dwt) for USD \n18.5 mill, while the 2006 Tsuneishi Cebu-built VINCA (52,447 dwt) was committed for region low-mid USD 12 \nmill. In the Handysize segment, Greek buyers paid USD 20 mill for the Japanese-built, open-hatch ASTRO \nPOLARIS (37,054 dwt, 2014 Onomichi) basis charter-free delivery within the year, while the one-year-older \nCETUS TIGER (37,059 dwt, 2013 Saiki) was sold for USD 18.3 mill and the Chinese-built BAO SHINE (32,203 \ndwt, 2013 JNS) for USD 13 mill. On the tanker front, the 2008 HMD-built LEON WISDOM (49,999 dwt) was \nsold for USD 26 mill, giving her sellers a profit of about USD 9.2 mill on the USD 16.8 mill they paid for her in \nJanuary. The 2005 Shin Kurushima-built ROSE MAKIS (45,838 dwt) was sold for USD 19 mill. In the chemical \nsegment, the stainless-steel FG ROTTERDAM (19,995 dwt, 2012 Usuki) changed hands for USD 24 mill. The \ndeal of the week was the en bloc sale of three 18,500 dwt chemical tanker resales, GOLDEN OAKTREE, \nGOLDEN PINE and GOLDEN POPLAR, due for delivery from Fujian Southeast Shipyard in 2027, at USD 36.5 \nmill each. \nFor a detailed review and additional information regarding this week's transactions, kindly refer to our \n\"representative sales\" section.",
    "demolition": "The 1998-built geared Handysize SPARROW (24,176 dwt, 5,618 ldt) was sold for demolition to \nPakistan at USD 500/LT. The Indian market softened slightly, although demand and pricing remain at similar \nlevels to last week. Bangladesh and Pakistan are the strongest destinations, with firm levels, while Turkey is \nholding stable. \nFor an overview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer on the next page:"
  },
